<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a>–<a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as British Civil Wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> were a series of linked wars from 1639 to 1653 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland, then ruled by Charles I. They included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, fought over religion, royal power and the relations between the three kingdoms. They ended with the execution of Charles I and the conquest of Scotland and Ireland by the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Wars of the Three Kingdoms" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Anthony Van Dyck (1599 - 1641) Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Anthony_Van_Dyck_-_Charles_I_(1600-49)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were separate kingdoms joined in a personal union when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> in 1603. His son Charles I faced disputes with the English Parliament over taxation, military expenditure and the limits of its role, and he held to the divine right of kings. Religion deepened the quarrel. English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> and Scottish Covenanters opposed his changes to the state churches, while in Ireland, the one kingdom with a Catholic majority, Catholics wanted an end to discrimination, more self-government and a reversal of land grants to Protestant settlers. The name itself appears first in a 1662 chronicle by James Heath, and historians use other labels as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a> of 1639 and 1640 began when Scottish Covenanters, who had signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a> of 1638 against religious innovations, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and briefly occupied northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. An Irish Catholic rebellion followed in 1641, with widespread assaults on Protestant communities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederate-ireland">Irish Catholic Confederation</a> came to hold most of Ireland. Because Parliament and the king each distrusted the other with an army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> began in 1642. The Covenanters joined Parliament in late 1643, and Charles surrendered to the Scottish army on 5 May 1646.</p><p>Fighting resumed in 1648 in the Second English Civil War, in which the New Model Army defeated the Royalists and the Scottish Engagers. After the army purged Parliament, the Rump Parliament approved the trial of Charles, who was executed on 30 January 1649, and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> a republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> landed in Ireland in August 1649 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a> at Dunbar on 3 September 1650. The last Royalist army, under Charles II, was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> on 3 September 1651.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were ruled by military governors, their parliaments were abolished, and in Ireland almost all land held by Catholics was confiscated, with Penal Laws restricting them further. After Cromwell died in 1658, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-monck">George Monck</a> marched from Scotland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in early 1660, and the Convention Parliament invited Charles II to return on 1 May 1660. The Restoration brought back the prewar constitutional position and the parliaments of Scotland and Ireland, and those held responsible for the regicide were executed or imprisoned. The issues of religion, the powers of Parliament and the relations between the kingdoms remained unresolved and re-emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wars of the Three Kingdoms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Reformation led by John Knox produced a Calvinist church that shaped Scottish society, education and ideas of resistance, and the National Covenant of 1638 helped to start the <b>Wars of the Three Kingdoms</b> that convulsed Britain and Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
